QEMU's `guestfwd=` gains a `unix:<path>` target: each TCP connection the
guest opens to server:port becomes a new connection to the host's Unix
socket at <path>, through libslirp's slirp_add_unix(). A chardev target
carries one connection for the machine's whole life, and `cmd:` spawns a
process; the player's SMB server (libsmb, track M23) wants ordinary
per-connection streams in its own process. `slirp_add_unix` is Unix-only
in libslirp 4.9.5, so on Windows the rule is refused.

